readings.c1s1:
{{smaller block|Now soon they reach Newcastle jail,
And to the pris'ner thus they call;
"Sleips thou, wakes thou, Jock o' the Side,
Or is thou wearied o' thy ''thrall?''"
{{right|''Jock o' the Side'' (Child's Ballads, VI. 84).}}}}

{{smaller block|I saw pale kings and princes too; . . .
They cried—"La Belle Dame sans Merci
Hath thee in ''thrall!''"
{{right|''Keats'', La Belle Dame sans Merci.}}}}

'''4.''' A shelf or stand; a stand for barrels. [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|The dairy ''thralls'' I might ha' wrote my name on 'em, when I come downstairs after my illness.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Adam Bede, vi. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Enslaved; bond; subjugated.

{{smaller block|Ther liberte loste, ther contre made ''thrall''
With that fers geant huge and comerous,
Horrible, myghty, strong, and orgulous.
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 4065.}}}}

{{smaller block|So the Philistines, the better to keep the Jews ''thrall'' and in subjection, utterly bereaved them of all manner weapon and artillery, and left them naked.
{{right|''Bp. Jewel'', Works, II. 672.}}}}

'''2.''' Figuratively, subject; enthralled.

{{smaller block|Disposeth ay youre hertes to withstonde
The feend that yow wolde make ''thrale'' and bonde.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Friar's Tale, l. 362.}}}}

{{smaller block|He cometh not of gentle blood
That to his coyne is ''thrall.''
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 103.}}}}

{{smaller block|We govern nature in opinions, but we are ''thrall'' unto her in necessity. ''Bacon'', Praise of Knowledge (ed. 1887).}}

[Obsolete or archaic in both uses.]

'''thrall''' (thrâl), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''thrallen''; < ''thrall'', ''n.''] '''1.''' To deprive of liberty; enslave.

readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|For more precyous Catelle ne gretter Ransoum ne myghte he put for us than his blessede Body, his precyous Blood, and his holy Lyf, that he ''thralled'' for us.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|My husband's brother had my son
''Thrall'd'' in his castle, and hath starved him dead.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Gareth and Lynette.}}}}

2. Figuratively, to put in subjection to some power or influence; enthrall.

{{smaller block|Love, which that so soone kan
The freedom of youre hertes to him ''thralle.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, i. 235.}}}}

{{smaller block|Not all thy manacles
Could fetter so my heeles, as this one word
Hath ''thrall'd'' my heart.
{{right|''Heywood'', Woman Killed with Kindness.}}}}

'''thraller''' (thrâ′lėr), ''n.'' [< ''thrall'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who thralls. ''Encyc. Dict.''

'''thralless†''' (thrâ′les), ''n.'' [ME., < ''thrall'' + ''-ess.''] A bondwoman. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|There [in Egypt] thow shalt be sold to thin enemyes, into thrallis and ''thrallessis.'' {{right|''Wyclif'', Deut. xxviii. 68.}}}}

'''thrallful''' (thrâl′fu̇l), ''a.'' [< ''thrall'' + ''-ful.''] Enthralled; slavish.

{{smaller block|Also the Lord accepted Iob, and staid
His ''Thrall-full'' State.
{{right|''Sylvester'', Job Triumphant, iv.}}}}

'''thrang'''<sup>1</sup> (thrang), ''n.'' A Scotch (and Middle English) form of ''throng''<sup>1</sup>.

'''thrang'''<sup>2</sup> (thrang), ''a.'' and ''adv.'' [A Scotch (and ME.) form of ''throng''<sup>2</sup>.] Crowded; much occupied; busy; intimate; thick.

{{smaller block|Twa dogs that were na ''thrang'' at hame
Forgather'd ance upon a time. {{right|''Burns'', Twa Dogs.}}}}

{{smaller block|It will be hard for you to fill her place, especially on sic a ''thrang'' day as this. {{right|''Scott'', Old Mortality, iv.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
'''{{smaller|Heads and thraws}}'''{{smaller|, lying side by side, the feet of the one by the head of the other.}}

'''thraw<sup>2</sup>''' (thrâ), ''n.'' and ''v.'' A Scotch form of ''throw''<sup>2</sup> for ''throe''<sup>1</sup>.—'''{{smaller|In the dead thraw}}'''{{smaller|, in the death-throes; in the last agonies: the phrase is also applied to any object regarded as neither dead nor alive, neither hot nor cold. ''Scott'', Guy Mannering, xxvii.}}

'''thraw<sup>3</sup>''', ''n.'' A Scotch form of ''throw''<sup>3</sup>.

'''thraward, thrawart''' (thrâ′wärd, -wärt), ''a.'' [Appar. < ''thraw''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ard'' (mixed with ''fraward'', ''froward'' (?)).] Cross-grained; perverse; stubborn; tough; also, reluctant. [Scotch.]

{{smaller block|I have kend the Law this mony a year, and mony a ''thrawart'' job I hae had wi' her first and last.
{{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, xiii.}}}}

'''thraw-crook''' (thrâ′kru̇k), ''n.'' See ''throw-crook'', 1.

'''thrawn''' (thrân), ''p. a.'' [A Sc. form of ''thrown;'' cf. ''thraw''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Twisted; wrenched; distorted; sprained: as, a ''thrawn'' stick; a ''thrawn'' foot.—'''2.''' Cross-grained; perverse; contrary or contradictory.

{{smaller block|"Of what are you made?" "Dirt" was the answer uniformly given. "Wull ye never learn to say dust, ye ''thrawn'' deevil?"
{{right|''Dr. J. Brown'', Marjorie Fleming.}}}}

'''thread''' (thred), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''thred;'' also ''threed'', whence, with shortened vowel, ''thrid;'' < ME. ''threed'', ''thred'', ''threde'', < AS. ''thrǣd'' = OFries. ''thrēd'' = MD. ''draed'', D. ''draad'' = OHG. MHG. ''drāt'', G. ''draht'', thread, wire, = Icel. ''thrādhr'' = Sw. ''tråd'' = Dan. ''traad'' = Goth. *''thrēths'' (not recorded), thread; lit. 'that which is twisted' (cf. ''twist'', ''twine'', thread); with formative ''-d'', < AS. ''thrāwan'', etc., twist, turn: see ''throw''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' A
